Previous 4.4 (SourceForge SVN trunk r1847) — hook the absolute-pointer control
channel into the build and into the emulation thread.

This patch is only the two hooks; the mechanism itself is the two new files
previous-abspointer.c -> src/abspointer.c and
previous-abspointer.h -> src/includes/abspointer.h, which must be copied into
the checkout before applying.

WHY THESE TWO INSERTION POINTS

  src/CMakeLists.txt   abspointer.c joins SOURCES, so the channel is compiled
                       into the `previous` binary and not carried as a script.

  Main_Loop()          AbsPointer_Init() binds the UNIX socket and starts the
                       listener thread once, at the same place the emulator
                       registers its own special event, i.e. after the machine
                       is initialised and before the CPU starts.

  Main_EventHandler()  AbsPointer_Poll() drains the channel on the EMULATION
                       thread, immediately after GuiEvent_EventHandler() —
                       which is where SDL's own key and mouse events are
                       drained — and before Timing_Sync(). That matters twice
                       over: guest RAM and the KMS registers are touched from
                       the same thread that runs the 68040, so there is no data
                       race with the CPU; and an absolute placement is
                       scheduled at exactly the same 200 Hz tick as a relative
                       SDL motion is today, so the mechanism adds no polling
                       latency that the shipped pointer path did not already
                       have. Measured on the tile clone: median input-to-photon
                       9.5-10.1 ms for both paths, |delta| <= 0.9 ms over three
                       interleaved rounds of n=24.

Apply with: patch -p0 -d <previous-code checkout> < this file

